From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Subject: [PATCH] DEBUG: ath11k: nss: disable the 4-address bookkeeping per peer (E86)

E86. The peer create message leaves the flags word at zero, so the
wifili firmware keeps 4-address/WDS state for every peer. Measured on
NSS.FW.12.2-156-MP.R: right after the first data frame touches a peer
the firmware emits NSS_WIFILI_WDS_ACTIVE_INFO whose error field carries
the poison-plus-0x800 signature (0x800, 0x4b4b0800, 0xb4b40800) and the
node's datapath stops - one RX delivery, no TX, control still ACKing.
The same signature was seen on an AP self peer (E63, which led to
999-949 disarming WDS backhaul on AP vdevs) and now on a STA's AP peer,
where nothing arms WDS from the host at all - the per-peer bookkeeping
is on by default.

The driver interface provides the opt out: set
NSS_WIFILI_PEER_MSG_DISABLE_4ADDR in the peer create flags.

Not for submission - this is a measurement.

--- a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/nss.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/nss.c
@@ -2751,6 +2751,15 @@
 	 */
 	peer_msg->peer_memory_size = WIFILI_NSS_PEER_BYTE_SIZE;
 
+	/* DEBUG E86: the message flags stay at the kzalloc zero, so the
+	 * firmware runs its 4-address/WDS bookkeeping for every peer. On this
+	 * blob that path emits NSS_WIFILI_WDS_ACTIVE_INFO with a poisoned
+	 * payload (0x...0800) right after the first data frame and the
+	 * datapath stops - measured identically on an AP self peer (E63) and
+	 * on a STA's AP peer (E80..E85). The header provides an opt out.
+	 */
+	peer_msg->flags = NSS_WIFILI_PEER_MSG_DISABLE_4ADDR;
+
 	msg_cb = (nss_wifili_msg_callback_t)ath11k_nss_wifili_event_receive;
 
 	nss_cmn_msg_init(&wlmsg->cm, ab->nss.if_num,
